import Foundation

// MARK: - Hearing God's Voice Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let hearingGodsVoiceContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 10:27",
            scriptureText: "My sheep listen to my voice; I know them, and they follow me.",
            devotionalTitle: "You Know His Voice",
            devotionalText: "Jesus says His sheep listen to His voice — not try to, not struggle to, but do. If you belong to Jesus, you have the capacity to hear Him. The issue is rarely that God isn't speaking; it's that we're not tuned in. Sheep learn their shepherd's voice through proximity and practice. The more time you spend with Jesus, the more naturally you'll recognize His voice.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, I am one of Your sheep. You know me, and I want to know Your voice better. Tune my ears to hear You above the noise. Help me listen — not just with my ears, but with my heart. I want to follow where You lead. Speak, Lord, and help me recognize it's You. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How confident are you that you can distinguish God's voice from your own thoughts?",
            actionSteps: ["Spend 10 minutes in silence and ask Jesus to speak — write down anything that comes to mind", "Read John 10:1-18 and notice how Jesus describes the relationship between shepherd and sheep"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Samuel 3:9-10",
            scriptureText: "So Eli told Samuel, 'Go and lie down, and if he calls you, say, \"Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening.\"' So Samuel went and lay down in his place. The Lord came and stood there, calling as at the other times, 'Samuel! Samuel!' Then Samuel said, 'Speak, for your servant is listening.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Speak, Lord",
            devotionalText: "Young Samuel heard God's voice three times before he recognized it. He needed Eli's help to understand what was happening. Sometimes we need mentors to help us recognize God's voice. And sometimes God keeps calling even when we don't respond right away. He's patient. The prayer 'Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening' is one of the most powerful prayers you can pray.",
            prayerText: "Speak, Lord, for Your servant is listening. I may not recognize Your voice every time, but I'm learning. Keep calling me. Keep speaking. Give me ears to hear and a heart that responds. Like Samuel, I position myself to listen. I am Your servant, and I am ready to hear. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Have you been hearing God but not recognizing it was Him?",
            actionSteps: ["Pray 'Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening' before your quiet time today", "Ask a mature believer to help you discern what God might be saying to you"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 46:10",
            scriptureText: "He says, 'Be still, and know that I am God; I will be exalted among the nations, I will be exalted in the earth.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Still Enough to Hear",
            devotionalText: "It's hard to hear a whisper in a hurricane. God often speaks in the quiet, and the only way to hear quiet is to be still. Stillness isn't laziness — it's discipline. In a world engineered to keep you distracted, choosing to be still is a radical act. God doesn't compete with your noise. He waits for your stillness. Create space, and His voice will fill it.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I choose to be still. Quiet the noise in my mind, my schedule, and my heart. I know that You are God — I don't need to strive or control. In this stillness, speak. I'm listening. Help me make stillness a regular part of my life, not just a rare event. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "When was the last time you were truly still — no phone, no noise, no agenda?",
            actionSteps: ["Set a timer for 10 minutes of complete silence and stillness before God", "Identify one source of noise in your life you can reduce to create more space for God's voice"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 30:21",
            scriptureText: "Whether you turn to the right or to the left, your ears will hear a voice behind you, saying, 'This is the way; walk in it.'",
            devotionalTitle: "This Is the Way",
            devotionalText: "God promises directional guidance — a voice that says 'this is the way.' It comes from behind, like a shepherd guiding from the back of the flock, gently redirecting when you veer off course. You don't need to see the whole map. You just need to hear 'this is the way' for the next step. God guides in real time, not just in advance.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I need to hear Your voice saying 'this is the way.' I'm at a crossroads and I need direction. Guide me from behind — redirect me when I veer off course. I don't need the whole map; I just need the next step. Speak clearly enough that I can follow. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Where do you need God's directional guidance right now?",
            actionSteps: ["Write down a specific decision you're facing and ask God to show you the way", "Pay attention to 'nudges' throughout the day — they might be God's gentle guidance"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Hebrews 1:1-2",
            scriptureText: "In the past God spoke to our ancestors through the prophets at many times and in various ways, but in these last days he has spoken to us by his Son.",
            devotionalTitle: "God Speaks in Many Ways",
            devotionalText: "God has never been limited to one communication method. He's spoken through prophets, dreams, burning bushes, still small voices, and ultimately through Jesus. He's creative in how He communicates. Don't limit God to one channel. He might speak through Scripture, a friend, circumstances, worship, nature, or a quiet impression on your heart. Stay open to His variety.",
            prayerText: "Lord, open my ears to hear You in all the ways You speak. Through Your Word, through people, through circumstances, through creation, through quiet impressions. I don't want to miss Your voice because I was only tuned to one channel. You are creative, and so is Your communication. Help me hear You everywhere. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What unexpected ways has God spoken to you in the past?",
            actionSteps: ["Pay attention to at least three different 'channels' today where God might be speaking", "Journal about a time God spoke to you in an unexpected way"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 3:5-6",
            scriptureText: "Trust in the Lord with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways submit to him, and he will make your paths straight.",
            devotionalTitle: "Trust Beyond Understanding",
            devotionalText: "Hearing God often requires trusting beyond what you understand. Your own understanding is limited, biased, and sometimes wrong. God sees the full picture. When His direction doesn't make logical sense, that's exactly when trust matters most. Submission to God's voice — even when it contradicts your analysis — is the pathway to straight paths.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I trust You with all my heart. I let go of my own understanding and submit to Yours. Make my paths straight. When Your voice leads me somewhere that doesn't make sense, help me follow anyway. My understanding is limited; Yours is infinite. I choose trust over analysis. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there something God is asking you to do that doesn't make sense to your own understanding?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one area where you've been leaning on your own understanding instead of trusting God", "Take one step of obedience today that requires trust beyond what you can see"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Jeremiah 33:3",
            scriptureText: "Call to me and I will answer you and tell you great and unsearchable things you do not know.",
            devotionalTitle: "He Has More to Tell You",
            devotionalText: "God has things He wants to reveal to you — great and unsearchable things you don't know yet. But there's a condition: call to Him. Ask. Seek. Knock. God doesn't broadcast His deepest truths to casual listeners. He shares them with those who earnestly seek Him. There are mysteries waiting to be uncovered, plans waiting to be revealed. Call to Him and prepare to be amazed.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I call to You. Answer me and show me great and unsearchable things I do not know. I want to go deeper with You — beyond surface-level faith into the depths of Your wisdom. Reveal what I haven't seen. Teach me what I don't know. I'm calling, and I'm ready to listen. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What 'great and unsearchable' things might God want to reveal to you if you asked?",
            actionSteps: ["Spend time in prayer specifically asking God to reveal something new to you", "Keep a 'God spoke' journal and record anything He reveals this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Acts 13:2",
            scriptureText: "While they were worshiping the Lord and fasting, the Holy Spirit said, 'Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Worship Opens Ears",
            devotionalText: "The early church heard the Holy Spirit's specific guidance during worship and fasting. There's a connection between worship and hearing God. When you worship, you shift your focus from yourself to God. Your spiritual ears open. The noise of your own agenda fades. Some of God's clearest words come in moments of devoted worship. If you want to hear God, start by worshiping Him.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I worship You — not to get something from You, but because You are worthy. And as I worship, open my ears to hear Your Spirit. Speak direction, correction, encouragement, or calling. I'm listening in the middle of worship. Let my praise create the atmosphere for Your voice. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Have you ever heard God clearly during a time of worship? What was that like?",
            actionSteps: ["Spend 15 minutes in uninterrupted worship and listen for God's voice at the end", "Try combining worship and journaling — worship, then write what comes to your heart"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 16:13",
            scriptureText: "But when he, the Spirit of truth, comes, he will guide you into all the truth. He will not speak on his own; he will speak only what he hears, and he will tell you what is yet to come.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Spirit Guides",
            devotionalText: "You're not trying to hear God alone — the Holy Spirit is your personal guide into truth. He takes what the Father says and delivers it to your heart. He guides, He reveals, He even tells you about the future. If you've been straining to hear God in your own effort, relax. The Spirit's job is to make God's voice clear to you. Partner with Him.",
            prayerText: "Holy Spirit, guide me into all truth today. I rely on You to make God's voice clear. I can't hear on my own — I need Your help. Speak to me what You hear from the Father. Guide my decisions, reveal hidden truth, and show me what's ahead. I partner with You in listening. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Are you relying on your own effort to hear God, or partnering with the Holy Spirit?",
            actionSteps: ["Before reading Scripture today, pray: 'Holy Spirit, guide me into truth as I read'", "Practice pausing throughout the day to ask the Spirit: 'What are You saying right now?'"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Habakkuk 2:1",
            scriptureText: "I will stand at my watch and station myself on the ramparts; I will look to see what he will say to me, and what answer I am to give to this complaint.",
            devotionalTitle: "Station Yourself to Listen",
            devotionalText: "Habakkuk didn't just pray and move on. He stationed himself — took a position, stayed put, and watched for God's response. Hearing God requires intentional positioning. You can't listen on the run. Sometimes you need to stop, plant yourself, and wait. God speaks to those who are positioned to receive. Station yourself today.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I station myself before You. I'm not rushing through prayer or moving on to the next thing. I'm staying right here, watching and listening for Your response. Speak to me in this place of waiting. I'll stay as long as it takes to hear from You. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you give God enough time to respond, or do you pray and immediately move on?",
            actionSteps: ["After prayer today, stay 5 extra minutes in silence waiting for God's response", "Create a consistent 'listening post' — a specific time and place where you regularly listen for God"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Deuteronomy 4:29",
            scriptureText: "But if from there you seek the Lord your God, you will find him if you seek him with all your heart and with all your soul.",
            devotionalTitle: "Wholehearted Seeking",
            devotionalText: "Half-hearted seeking produces half-hearted results. God promises He will be found — but by those who seek with all their heart and soul. This means bringing your full attention, your genuine desire, and your persistent effort to the search. God isn't hiding. He's waiting to be found by those who want Him badly enough to seek wholeheartedly.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I seek You with all my heart and all my soul. Not casually, not half-heartedly, but with everything I have. I want to find You. I want to hear You. I bring my full attention and my deepest desire. You promised I would find You if I sought You wholeheartedly. Here I am, seeking. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "On a scale of 1-10, how wholeheartedly are you seeking God right now? What would a 10 look like?",
            actionSteps: ["Remove one distraction from your seeking time and give God your full attention", "Set aside an extended time this week — 30 minutes or more — dedicated entirely to seeking God"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Kings 19:11-12",
            scriptureText: "The Lord said, 'Go out and stand on the mountain in the presence of the Lord, for the Lord is about to pass by.' Then a great and powerful wind tore the mountains apart and shattered the rocks before the Lord, but the Lord was not in the wind. After the wind there was an earthquake, but the Lord was not in the earthquake. After the earthquake came a fire, but the Lord was not in the fire. And after the fire came a gentle whisper.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Gentle Whisper",
            devotionalText: "Elijah expected God in the spectacular — the wind, earthquake, and fire. But God was in the gentle whisper. This is perhaps the most important lesson about hearing God: He often speaks quietly. If you're waiting for thunder, you'll miss the whisper. God's voice is more often a gentle impression than a dramatic event. Get quiet enough to hear the whisper.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I stop looking for the spectacular and start listening for the whisper. Quiet my heart so I can hear Your gentle voice. I've been waiting for thunder when You've been whispering all along. Tune my ears to the still, small voice. I'm listening for the whisper. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Have you been looking for God in the dramatic while missing His gentle whisper?",
            actionSteps: ["Find the quietest place you can, sit in complete silence, and listen for God's whisper", "At the end of each day this week, reflect: 'Where did I sense God's gentle whisper today?'"]
        ),
    ]
}
